      title: "From Manhattan to the Forecastle",
      body: `## A family fallen

**Herman Melville** was born in **New York City** on **August 1, 1819**, the third of eight children of **Allan Melvill**, an importer of French luxury goods, and **Maria Gansevoort Melvill**. Both his grandfathers were heroes of the Revolution. **Thomas Melvill** had taken part in the Boston Tea Party; **Peter Gansevoort** had defended Fort Stanwix against the British in 1777. The family lived well, with servants, in fashionable Manhattan houses.

But Allan Melvill was a poor businessman who lived on borrowed money. In **1830**, his business collapsed, and the family fled their creditors to **Albany**, where Maria's wealthy relatives lived. Two years later, in **January 1832**, Allan died, after a feverish illness that ended in delirium, deeply in debt. Herman was twelve. The family, now dependent on the Gansevoorts, sank into genteel poverty. (Maria added the final "e" to the family name around this time.) Herman left school and worked as a bank clerk, a clerk in his brother's fur store, a farmhand, and a schoolteacher.

## Liverpool

In **June 1839**, at the age of nineteen, unable to find steady work, Melville signed on as a "boy," the lowest rank of seaman, on a merchant ship, the *St. Lawrence*, bound for **Liverpool**. The voyage took four months. In Liverpool, he saw the slums and docks of the great industrial port, and beggars dying in the streets. He later turned the experience into his novel ***Redburn*** (1849), in which a genteel young man discovers the brutality of the sailor's life and of poverty.

## The Acushnet

After another period of unsuccessful schoolteaching, and a trip to the Mississippi, Melville signed on, on **January 3, 1841**, as a common seaman aboard the **whaler *Acushnet***, sailing from **Fairhaven**, across the harbor from **New Bedford**, Massachusetts, the greatest whaling port in the world. Whaling voyages lasted three or four years; the crew were paid not wages, but a "**lay**," a fraction of the profits from the oil. Melville's lay was 1/175.

The *Acushnet* sailed around Cape Horn into the Pacific. During the voyage, at sea, Melville met a whaleman who had sailed with the son of **Owen Chase**, first mate of the whaler ***Essex***. In **November 1820**, the *Essex* had been rammed and sunk by an enraged **sperm whale** in the Pacific; her crew had drifted for three months in open boats, and the survivors had been reduced to cannibalism. Melville borrowed the son's copy of Chase's *Narrative* (1821) and read it at sea. "The reading of this wondrous story upon the landless sea, & close to the very latitude of the shipwreck had a surprising effect upon me," he later wrote. Whalemen also told stories of a giant white sperm whale, nicknamed **Mocha Dick**, after the island of Mocha off Chile, who had fought off many attacks; an article about him by **Jeremiah N. Reynolds** had appeared in the *Knickerbocker Magazine* in 1839.

## Typee

In **July 1842**, after eighteen months at sea, the *Acushnet* anchored at **Nuku Hiva** in the **Marquesas Islands** of the South Pacific, which France had just annexed. Melville and a shipmate, **Richard Tobias Greene** ("Toby"), deserted, fleeing inland across the mountains. They came down into a valley inhabited by the **Taipi** (Typee), a people reputed to be cannibals. Melville lived among them for about **four weeks** (he later claimed four months), cared for by a family, and treated with kindness, though he feared that he would be eaten. Toby left to seek help and did not return. Melville escaped on an Australian whaler, the *Lucy Ann*.

## Tahiti, Hawaii, and the navy

The *Lucy Ann* was a miserable ship, with a sick captain and a mutinous crew. At **Tahiti**, Melville and others refused to sail on, and were briefly imprisoned by the British consul in a loosely guarded jail, from which they wandered freely. He drifted around Tahiti and the neighboring island of Moorea, then shipped on another whaler, the *Charles and Henry*, and was discharged in **Hawaii** in **1843**. There he worked as a clerk and a pinsetter in a bowling alley in Honolulu. In **August 1843**, he enlisted as an ordinary seaman in the **U.S. Navy** aboard the frigate ***United States***, and sailed home, arriving in Boston in **October 1844**. He had been away nearly four years.

## The education of the sea

Melville later wrote, in *Moby-Dick*, that "**a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard**." Unlike Emerson, Thoreau, and Hawthorne, he never attended college. His education came from the sea, from the forecastle, from the men of every race and nation he worked beside, and from the books he devoured after he returned. He saw the world from below, as a common laborer, and the experience gave his work a democratic sympathy, a sense of the brutality of power, and a knowledge of the wider world that none of his New England contemporaries could match.

## The Pacific world

The Pacific Melville saw in the early 1840s was being transformed. American and British whalers, traders, and missionaries were arriving in growing numbers; France annexed the Marquesas in 1842 and made Tahiti a protectorate the same year; Hawaii's kingdom was negotiating with the Western powers to keep its independence. Epidemics brought by foreigners were devastating island populations. Melville witnessed this collision of worlds as a common sailor and a deserter, from below rather than from the deck of a warship, and it shaped his lifelong suspicion of what he called "civilization" and its missionaries.

Many whalemen deserted in the Pacific, as Melville did; the hard life and long voyages made desertion common, and captains often recruited islanders to replace them.

> **Pause and consider.** Melville wrote that "a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard." What kinds of knowledge does a forecastle teach that a college does not?`,
