      title: "Typee to Mardi: The Young Author",
      body: `## Typee

When Melville came home, his family encouraged him to write down the stories of his adventures, which he told brilliantly. The result was ***Typee: A Peep at Polynesian Life*** (**1846**), published first in London by John Murray and then in New York by Wiley and Putnam. It was an immediate success. Readers were enthralled by its account of an earthly paradise, its beautiful young woman, **Fayaway**, who sails a canoe using her robe as a sail, and its frisson of danger from cannibals.

But *Typee* was more than an adventure story. Melville praised the Typees' way of life—their freedom from the anxiety of money, their communal property, their harmony—and contrasted it, with bitter irony, with the corruption of so-called civilization. "The term 'Savage' is, I conceive, often misapplied, and indeed, when I consider the vices, cruelties, and enormities of every kind that spring up in the tainted atmosphere of a feverish civilization, I am inclined to think that so far as the relative wickedness of the parties is concerned, four or five Marquesan Islanders sent to the United States as Missionaries might be quite as useful as an equal number of Americans dispatched to the Islands in a similar capacity." He attacked the Christian **missionaries** in the Pacific, and the French annexation of the Marquesas. The American edition was expurgated of the passages most hostile to the missionaries.

## Was it true?

Many readers doubted that *Typee* was a true story. Then, in **July 1846**, a letter appeared in a Buffalo newspaper from **Richard Tobias Greene**, Toby himself, confirming Melville's account and explaining that he had been prevented from returning to the valley. Melville met Toby and wrote a "Sequel" for the revised edition. Modern scholars have shown that Melville drew heavily on published travel books for his ethnographic details, and that he exaggerated the length of his stay; but the core of the story was true.

## Omoo and marriage

Melville followed with ***Omoo*** (**1847**), a lighthearted account of his adventures in Tahiti, which was also successful. On **August 4, 1847**, he married **Elizabeth Shaw**, the daughter of **Lemuel Shaw**, chief justice of the Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court and an old friend of Melville's father. The couple settled in New York, sharing a house with Melville's brother, his mother, and his sisters. They had four children.

## Mardi

Melville's third book, ***Mardi: and a Voyage Thither*** (**1849**), began as another South Sea adventure, but turned into something else entirely: a vast, sprawling allegory, in which the narrator and his companions sail from island to island of an imaginary archipelago, representing the nations of the world, in search of a mysterious maiden named **Yillah**, discussing philosophy, politics, and religion. Melville had been reading voraciously—Shakespeare, Rabelais, Sir Thomas Browne, Burton's *Anatomy of Melancholy*—and his ambition had outrun his readers. *Mardi* was a commercial and critical failure. "It is my earnest desire to write those sort of books which are said to 'fail,'" he wrote, half in jest, to his father-in-law.

## Redburn and White-Jacket

Needing money for his growing family, Melville wrote two more conventional books in rapid succession, in the summer of **1849**: ***Redburn*** (1849), based on his voyage to Liverpool, and ***White-Jacket*** (1850), based on his year in the navy. *White-Jacket* is a powerful attack on the brutality of naval discipline, especially **flogging**; it was distributed to members of Congress, and contributed to the abolition of flogging in the U.S. Navy in **September 1850**. Melville dismissed both books in a letter: "They are two *jobs*, which I have done for money—being forced to it, as other men are to sawing wood."

## Shakespeare

In **1849**, Melville bought a large-print edition of **Shakespeare**, and read it with passionate excitement, especially *King Lear*, *Hamlet*, *Macbeth*, and *Timon of Athens*. His copy, heavily marked, survives. "Dolt & ass that I am I have lived more than 29 years, & until a few days ago, never made close acquaintance with the divine William," he wrote to a friend. "Ah, he's full of sermons-on-the-mount, and gentle, aye, almost as Jesus." Shakespeare showed Melville what literature could do: how it could explore the darkest truths, in language of extraordinary power, through characters driven to madness by their own greatness. The influence of Shakespeare's tragic heroes, especially Lear, would shape the figure of Ahab.

## The lecture of the world

Melville's reading in these years was enormous and eclectic. He read philosophy (Plato, Spinoza, Kant, through Coleridge), theology (the Bible, Calvin, Pierre Bayle's skeptical *Dictionary*), science (Darwin's *Voyage of the Beagle*), and the vast literature of whaling. He went to Europe in **late 1849** to sell *White-Jacket* to a London publisher, and returned with books. Like Emerson, he believed that the writer must think for himself; unlike Emerson, he was drawn to the dark questions—evil, suffering, the silence of God—which Emerson's optimism seemed to him to evade. His surviving copies of Emerson's essays are full of pencil marks, some admiring and some impatient, especially beside Emerson's more serene claims about evil and the goodness of the universe. After hearing him lecture in 1849, he wrote to a friend: "I love all men who *dive*. Any fish can swim near the surface, but it takes a great whale to go down stairs five miles or more." Yet he found in Emerson "a gaping flaw": the insinuation that, had he been present at the creation, he "might have offered some valuable suggestions."

Melville's earliest books were also his most profitable. He would never again earn from his writing what *Typee* and *Omoo* had brought him, and their success shaped what his publishers and readers expected of him.

> **Pause and consider.** Melville said he wished to "write those sort of books which are said to 'fail.'" What might a writer gain by aiming at failure?`,
